.TH BOOTLORE 1 "November 2026" "bootlore" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bootlore \- a readable observer of Linux boot logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bootlore
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B bootlore
.B help
|
.B version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bootlore
reads boot\-related events from the systemd journal and turns them into a
readable, chronological account of system startup.
It complements (and does not replace) tools like
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR dmesg (1)
and
.BR systemd\-analyze (1):
instead of raw logs, it offers interpretation, classification and educational
explanations.
.PP
BootLore is read\-only and non\-intrusive: it never modifies system
configuration, never requires root, and only sees journal entries the invoking
user is allowed to read.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS collect
Print a boot's events.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE
Read a captured journal export (as produced by
.BR "journalctl \-o json" )
from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B \-\-journal
Read directly from the system journal, even when stdin is piped.
.TP
.BI \-\-boot= OFFSET
Which boot to read, as a
.B journalctl \-b
offset:
.B 0
(current, the default),
.B \-1
(previous), and so on.
.TP
.BI \-\-category= LIST
Keep only events of the given subsystems.
See
.BR FILTERING .
.TP
.BI \-\-view= VIEW
Event view:
.B log
(the default \(em original messages, verbatim) or
.B human
(BootLore's interpretation).
.SS live
Replay the current boot's existing events, then follow the journal live.
Stops cleanly on SIGINT/SIGTERM.
.TP
.BI \-\-boot= OFFSET
Which boot to watch (default: current).
.TP
.BI \-\-category= LIST
Keep only events of the given subsystems.
See
.BR FILTERING .
.TP
.BI \-\-view= VIEW
log (default) or human.
.SS timeline
Show the boot as a chronological timeline: every event is placed at its
monotonic offset since boot (for example
.BR +1.200s ),
ordered chronologically.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET ", " \-\-category= LIST
Same sources, boot selection and category filter as
.BR collect .
.TP
.BI \-\-view= VIEW
human (the default) or log.
.SS summary
Aggregate a boot into a single overview: the number of events, how many were
normal, warnings and failures, the observed wall\-clock window, how far into the
boot the events reached, and a breakdown by subsystem. Honours the same sources
and category filter as
.BR collect ,
so the overview can be narrowed to, for example, only storage events.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET ", " \-\-category= LIST
Same sources, boot selection and category filter as
.BR collect .
.SS services
Diagnose systemd services for a boot: the units that failed (with their failure
message and offset), and the service startup timings BootLore could reconstruct
by pairing each
.B Starting
message with its
.B Started
or
.B Finished
message, slowest first. Only measured spans are reported; BootLore never guesses
a duration.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET ", " \-\-category= LIST
Same sources, boot selection and category filter as
.BR collect .
.SS timing
Report a boot's duration and timing. When systemd logged its authoritative
startup summary
.RB ( "Startup finished in ... = ..." ),
BootLore shows that total and its per\-phase breakdown
.RB ( firmware ,
.BR loader ,
.BR kernel ,
.BR userspace );
otherwise it falls back to the largest monotonic offset observed and marks the
total as observed rather than reported, so it never presents an estimate as a
fact. It also lists the boot targets reached
.RB ( "Reached target ..." )
at their offset since boot.
.PP
Timing reports the system boot only; the per\-user manager's own summary and
session targets are ignored.
If BootLore can see only your user\-session journal \(em usually because your
account is not in the
.B systemd\-journal
group \(em it cannot measure the system boot and prints a note on standard
error explaining how to fix it.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET
Same sources and boot selection as
.BR collect .
There is no
.B \-\-category
filter: dropping the startup summary line would corrupt the totals.
.SS compare
Contrast two boots: a current (later) boot against an earlier baseline. Answers
"how does this boot compare to the previous one?" by diffing their duration
(faster or slower), their event, warning and failure counts, and their failed
units, sorted into those that were fixed, those that newly regressed and those
still failing. A duration delta is flagged
.B observed
when either side's total came from the largest observed offset rather than
systemd's startup summary. When both boots carry systemd's startup summary, the
duration is broken down per phase
.RB ( firmware ,
.BR loader ,
.BR kernel ,
.BR initrd ,
.BR userspace )
so a firmware or boot-loader slowdown is never mistaken for a Linux regression; a
phase measured on only one side shows
.B n/a
for the other. Transient
.B run-*
units (as created by
.B systemd-run
and
.BR run0 )
are named uniquely per invocation, so they are kept out of the failed-unit diff
and merely counted per side. Long 32-character boot IDs are shortened to their
first twelve characters so the columns stay aligned. Unlike the other commands,
.B compare
does not read standard input: two boots cannot share one pipe.
.TP
.BI \-\-boot= OFFSET
The current (later) boot to read, as a
.B journalctl \-b
offset (default:
.BR 0 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-against= OFFSET
The baseline (earlier) boot to read, as a
.B journalctl \-b
offset (default:
.BR \-1 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE
Read the current boot's journal JSON from
.I FILE
instead of the journal.
.TP
.BI \-\-against\-input= FILE
Read the baseline boot's journal JSON from
.I FILE
instead of the journal.
.SS report
Assemble a boot's full diagnosis \(em its
.B summary ,
its
.B timing
and its
.B services
report \(em into a single document and render it in the requested format. The
figures are exactly those the individual commands report. Like
.B timing
it covers the whole boot and so takes no
.B \-\-category
filter: a holistic diagnosis of a single subsystem would mislead, and filtering
out events would corrupt the timing totals. If BootLore can see only your
user\-session journal it prints the same note on standard error as
.BR timing .
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET
Same sources and boot selection as
.BR collect .
.TP
.BI \-\-format= FMT
Report format:
.B text
(the default \(em the three console sub\-reports under a provenance header),
.B markdown
(a shareable document with headings and tables, for pasting into a bug report)
or
.B json
(a stable, documented schema for tooling and archival).
.TP
.BI \-\-output= FILE
Write the report to
.I FILE
instead of standard output, confirming the path on standard error. This is the
intended way to export a diagnosis.
.SS doctor
Check whether BootLore can do its job on this system: run a few small probes
against the live journal backend and report them as checks. It answers questions
like "why does
.B summary
see nothing" before they are asked. The checks are:
.IP journalctl 4
the journal backend can be run at all;
.IP journal 4
the system journal is readable (only your own user\-session journal is visible
otherwise);
.IP boot\-events 4
the current boot yields events;
.IP boots 4
how many boots the journal recorded (at least two are needed by
.BR compare ).
With no flags at all, doctor always probes the live system; piping a captured
export at it changes nothing. Each failed check is followed by a hint
suggesting a fix (for example joining the
.B systemd\-journal
group). The exit code is 0 when every check passes (warnings allowed) and 1 as
soon as one fails, so scripts can rely on it.
.SS explain
Print events with contextual, educational explanations indented under each
one, at the requested depth:
.B overview
(plain language),
.B technical
(the default: the underlying mechanism) or
.B in\-depth
(full educational context, including how to investigate).
Events without a known explanation are still listed; BootLore never invents
causes.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET ", " \-\-category= LIST
Same sources, boot selection and category filter as
.BR collect .
.TP
.BI \-\-level= LEVEL
overview, technical (default) or in\-depth.
.SS errors
Print only noteworthy events: warnings and failures. Defaults to the human
view; prints an explicit notice when nothing noteworthy is found.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= FILE ", " \-\-journal ", " \-\-boot= OFFSET ", " \-\-category= LIST
Same sources, boot selection and category filter as
.BR collect .
.TP
.BI \-\-view= VIEW
human (the default) or log.
.SS tui
Start the interactive boot monitor: full\-screen, monochrome\-safe, reads the
requested boot and then follows it live. Both stdin and stdout must be a
terminal; without one BootLore exits with code 1 and a clear message.
.TP
.BI \-\-boot= OFFSET
Which boot to watch (default: current).
.TP
.BI \-\-category= LIST
Keep only events of the given subsystems (see
.BR FILTERING ).
The filter can also be edited interactively.
.TP
Interactive keys:
1 / 2 switch LOG / HUMAN, Enter detail of the selected event ;
Tab cycle the explanation level (overview, technical, in\-depth) ;
j/k or arrows navigate (they scroll the explanation while the detail is open), g/G top/bottom, pgup/pgdn page ;
/ filter by category, Esc clear filter or close the detail ;
q or Ctrl\-C quit. All output is plain text; selection and the status bar use
reverse video only, which stays readable on monochrome terminals.
.SS version, help
Print the version, or the built\-in usage summary.
.SH SOURCES
Commands that read events choose their source by precedence:
.B \-\-input
first, then piped standard input, then the system journal. In a terminal,
.B bootlore collect
therefore reads the current boot from journald without further flags;
.B \-\-journal
forces the journal even when stdin is piped.
.SH FILTERING
.B \-\-category
takes a comma\-separated list of subsystems, case\-insensitive, and keeps only
the events BootLore classified into one of them:
.BR kernel ,
.BR hardware ,
.BR storage ,
.BR network ,
.BR systemd ,
.BR security ,
.BR desktop
and
.BR other .
The special name
.B unclassified
selects the events BootLore could not classify, which shows exactly what its
classification does not cover.
.PP
Filters compose: with
.BR errors ,
.B \-\-category
narrows the warnings and failures to the requested subsystems. An unknown name
is a usage error and lists the valid ones.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including an empty boot \(em nothing noteworthy is not an error).
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure: journal backend missing or failed, or malformed input.
A hint is printed on standard error when journalctl is unavailable.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown command, unknown flag, invalid view, level or category.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Read the current boot from the journal, as readable descriptions:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore collect \-\-view human
.fi
.RE
.PP
Follow only the network events as they happen:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore live \-\-category network
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what the previous boot got wrong in storage and hardware:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore errors \-\-boot \-1 \-\-category storage,hardware
.fi
.RE
.PP
Study how a boot works, in depth:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore explain \-\-level in\-depth
.fi
.RE
.PP
Get a one\-screen overview of the current boot:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore summary
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find out which services failed or were slow to start:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore services
.fi
.RE
.PP
See how long the boot took, phase by phase:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore timing
.fi
.RE
.PP
See whether this boot got faster or more reliable than the previous one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore compare
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export the current boot's full diagnosis as JSON for tooling:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootlore report \-\-format json \-\-output boot\-report.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Analyse a captured export without touching the live journal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl \-b \-o json | bootlore timeline
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
BootLore observes only what the systemd journal retained and what the invoking
user may read. It cannot guarantee observation from the first kernel
instruction: some early kernel or initramfs events may be missing, and live
observation begins only when BootLore starts. Live monitoring requires
.BR journalctl (1)
from systemd. See
.BR systemd\-journal (8)
for access\-level configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR systemd\-analyze (1),
.BR dmesg (1),
.BR systemd\-journal (8)
